### Next Steps for Part VII

### Next Steps

Part VII documents the formal reduction of twelve domain-level theories into compositions of Tier 1 neural mechanisms. Three forward-looking research directions complete and extend this reduction program.

First, **elevation and operationalization of "super-templates" (IC2 and AX4)** as formally indexed cross-reference entries. Two templates recurred across five or more independent domain-panel reductions: IC2 (Body Budget Prediction Error — interoceptive mismatch between predicted and actual body state) and AX4 (Perceived Control and Predictability — the explicit appraisal of agency over environmental conditions). IC2 appeared as a constitutive mechanism in thermal-comfort adaptations (MAT), stress-recovery (SRT), social-crowding responses (SOC), acoustic discomfort (AX1), and creative cognitive-demand management (CREA1). AX4 appeared in stress-recovery (SRT), spatial-legibility wayfinding (SC1), work-environment control (SOC3), and privacy regulation (SOC2). The convergence across independent panels suggests these are not domain-specific templates but rather **meta-mechanisms** operating across multiple domains. The proposition is to: (a) index IC2 and AX4 separately in the template library with a special "SUPER_TEMPLATE" metadata tag, (b) create a cross-reference table documenting which domain templates depend on or invoke IC2/AX4, (c) parameterize IC2 and AX4 at a higher level of abstraction than domain-specific instantiations (e.g., body-budget prediction error as a general construct, with domain-specific instantiations: thermal-mismatch PE, social-distance PE, acoustic-discomfort PE), (d) estimate the aggregate influence of IC2 and AX4 across the template library, and (e) test whether explicitly modeling IC2/AX4 as superordinate mechanisms reduces template intercorrelation and improves out-of-sample prediction. This upgrade would transform the template library from a flat collection of 103 independent mechanisms into a hierarchically structured system with super-mechanisms governing multiple domains. Implementation: 6–8 weeks of template re-indexing and cross-reference linking.

Second, **completing deferred candidates and resolving rejected candidates** by clarifying template-coverage requirements. Four theories are held in DEFERRED status pending expansion of the template library: (i) BRECVEMA (eight music-emotion mechanisms: Brain stem REflex, Emotional Contagion, Visual imagery, Episodic memory, Musical expectancy, Aesthetic judgments) — awaiting full M-series (MUSIC-I) template calibration and integration; (ii) Flow Theory (Csikszentmihalyi) — awaiting formal integration of CREA (creative cognition) templates and explicit-processing (IE-DPT) parametrization of challenge-skill balance; (iii) Adaptive Thermal Comfort — awaiting field validation of the MAT-series templates in naturally ventilated buildings; (iv) Narrative Attachment (newly proposed) — awaiting template development for biographical and cultural meaning-making in place experience. For each deferred candidate, establish a clear **target completion date** and required intermediate steps. BRECVEMA completion requires calibration of 6–8 music-emotion templates from MUSIC-I panel work (estimated Q3–Q4 2026); Flow Theory completion requires integration of explicit-processing modulation with the challenge-skill-balance construct (estimated Q2–Q3 2026); Adaptive Thermal Comfort completion requires 2–3 field studies in naturally ventilated buildings (estimated Q3 2026–Q1 2027); Narrative Attachment requires new template development in the place-attachment and biographical domains (estimated Q4 2026–Q2 2027). Conversely, six theories were rejected in the Expansion Panel: Proxemics (reduces to Privacy Regulation), Cognitive Map Theory (is SN itself), Chronobiology (is CB itself), CPTED (confounded with environmental variables), Allesthesia (is single mechanism, not theory), and Mehrabian-Russell PAD model (reduces without residual to IC + NM + DP). For rejected candidates, document **why the rejection decision was made** in a "Rejected Theories Epistemology" appendix: not all reductions are *equal*, and documenting the criteria distinguishing reducible from irreducible theories (and theories from mechanisms) strengthens the system's epistemic rigor. The philosophical principle is that **a theory reduces to T1 if and only if (a) its core constructs can be decomposed into T1 mechanisms, (b) the reduction explains ≥60% of the theory's explanatory scope, and (c) residuals are schema gaps rather than genuine irreducibility**. Documenting this principle across all candidates (accepted, deferred, rejected) creates a principled theoretical boundary.

Third, **incorporating practitioner-generated implicit theories** through structured knowledge engineering. Environmental professionals (architects, landscape architects, building scientists, urban planners, environmental psychologists) operate with implicit theories about why buildings work — explanatory models that guide their design decisions but that are often unarticulated and unsystematized. A structured **practitioner knowledge-engineering study** would: (a) conduct open-ended interviews with 40–60 design professionals asking "What makes a building feel good?" and "How do you explain why some spaces work well and others fail?"; (b) extract recurring conceptual structures, metaphors, and causal narratives (e.g., "coherence," "transparency," "nesting," "sequence," "rhythm," "material authenticity," "craft quality"); (c) map these practitioner constructs against existing T1.5 theories and T2 templates to identify coverage; (d) for practitioner constructs not well-covered by existing theories, evaluate whether they represent genuinely new theoretical domains (e.g., "craft quality" as a dimension of material engagement distinct from texture and thermal properties); (e) formally reduce those that meet structural criteria into candidate T1.5 status; (f) test candidate reductions against practitioner experience through vignette studies ("Do buildings scoring high on this theory match your intuitions about quality?"). This honours the principle that theoretical knowledge is distributed across expert communities (neuroscientists, environmental psychologists, and designers all hold valuable knowledge) and that the ATLAS system should integrate practitioner expertise rather than dismissing it as unscientific. Expected outcome: 2–4 new candidate T1.5 theories emerging from practitioner knowledge, to be evaluated alongside the current twelve for possible formal inclusion. Timeline: 4–6 months for knowledge engineering plus 3–6 months for candidate theory development and vignette-study validation.

